section.para:
StemmerFactory captures a stemmer configuration once and mints configured Stemmer instances on demand. It is a plain supplier of stemmers, not a BaseToolFactory loaded from a model manifest. The English Snowball factory needs no external data: StemmerFactoryUsageExampleTest asserts the behavior shown here.
StemmerFactory factory = new SnowballStemmerFactory(SnowballStemmer.ALGORITHM.ENGLISH); Stemmer stemmer = factory.newStemmer();
stemmer.stem("running"); // "run" stemmer.stemAll("running"); // ["run"]

opennlp.tools.stemmer.hunspell loads a user-supplied .aff affix file and its .dic word list. OpenNLP bundles no dictionary data; dictionaries are downloaded separately, and each states its own license. Retain the dictionary's copyright notices and license text with downloaded files. OpenNLP's Apache License does not relicense those files. The dictionary is immutable and safe to share; HunspellStemmerFactory creates a fresh stemmer per call. HunspellManualExampleTest asserts the behavior shown here.
HunspellDictionary dictionary = HunspellDictionary.load( Path.of("dictionary.aff"), Path.of("dictionary.dic")); Stemmer stemmer = new HunspellStemmerFactory(dictionary).newStemmer();
stemmer.stem("workers"); // "work" stemmer.stem("table"); // "table" (unknown vocabulary is unchanged)
The stems above are those of the project-authored miniature dictionary the test loads, which lists work with an agentive and a plural suffix; the test asserts the same stem for worker. Which stem a published dictionary yields for a given form is decided by that dictionary. Acquisition helpers and the supported affix feature set live in dev/README-hunspell-dictionaries.md. An opt-in catalog download accepts an application-supplied DictionaryCatalog, needs -Dopennlp.download.remote=true, and fetches through the digest-verified ResourceInstaller path. A file that already exists in the target is not replaced. Remove old files before refreshing a dictionary. Loading uses HunspellDictionary.LoadMode.STRICT by default. Unsupported affix directives cause an IOException with the directive name and source line. File-based loading also includes the path. This applies to ICONV, OCONV, COMPLEXPREFIXES, COMPOUNDRULE, IGNORE, KEEPCASE, and unknown directive names. Such dictionaries may be valid Hunspell input; the error identifies behavior that OpenNLP does not support. Metadata and suggestion settings that do not affect stemming are ignored. Applications can select ALLOW_PARTIAL to use the supported rules and inspect skipped directives:
HunspellDictionary partial = HunspellDictionary.load( Path.of("dictionary.aff"), Path.of("dictionary.dic"), HunspellDictionary.LoadMode.ALLOW_PARTIAL); for (HunspellDictionary.UnsupportedDirective diagnostic : partial.getUnsupportedDirectives()) { System.err.println(diagnostic.directive() + " at " + diagnostic.source() + ":" + diagnostic.lineNumber()); }
The diagnostic list is immutable and contains the first source location for each unsupported directive, in file order. Partial loading does not apply conversion, casing, or compound behavior from skipped directives. Dictionary morphology fields are ignored in both modes. Strict affix loading does not establish complete Hunspell compatibility. FLAG and AF declarations apply to the complete affix file, including rules listed before those declarations. Parsing rejects malformed text in parsed rules, invalid counts, numeric flags outside the range 1 through 65000, and COMPOUNDMIN values that cannot be doubled safely. Comments and unused metadata may retain a legacy encoding. Default and long flag modes preserve raw one-byte flag values in UTF-8 files. Compound length and boundary checks count Unicode code points. A rule that strips a whole stem applies only when the affix file declares FULLSTRIP, as in Hunspell itself. Each affix or dictionary stream is rejected when it exceeds HunspellDictionary.MAX_STREAM_BYTES (64 MiB).
